mixin () {
  /// Static Markdown description of this backend's public API.
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Sensor Monitoring Backend\n\nThis canister simulates a hardware sensor rig and serves live temperature, gas,\nand humidity readings for real-time monitoring. Readings drift over time within\nplausible ranges; no real hardware is connected and there is no manual entry\npath. Each reading carries a per-sensor status and an overall system status.\n\n## Public methods\n\n### `getCurrentReading() : async ?Reading` (query)\nReturns the most recent persisted reading, or `null` before the first\nsimulation step has run. Poll this for the live display.\n\n### `getRecentReadings(limit : Nat) : async [Reading]` (query)\nReturns up to `limit` readings, **newest first**. The retained window is\nbounded (60 readings); requesting a larger `limit` simply returns the whole\nwindow. Use this for the trend chart and the timestamped log.\n\n### `getThresholds() : async Thresholds` (query)\nReturns the predefined per-sensor threshold bands and the fluctuation delta\nused to derive status. These are fixed constants; they are not configurable.\n\n### `advanceSimulation() : async Reading` (update)\nAdvances the simulation by one step, persists the new reading, and returns it.\nThis is the only method that mutates state.\n\n### `getApiDoc() : async Text` (query)\nReturns this document.\n\n### `schema()` / `execute(...)` (OQL)\nThe persisted reading history is exposed through the Caffeine Object Query\nLayer as the `reading` entity, so it can be queried in natural language or via\nstructured JSON. The entity is world-readable (`.public_()`), matching the\npublic read methods above.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\nEvery method listed above is callable by **any caller, including anonymous\nones**. There is no sign-in requirement, no owner, and no admin-only surface on\nthis canister. The reading history is public data.\n\nThe app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at\n`/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding\nthe user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app\nprincipal against that origin (for example\n`icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the\nuser's full authority in this app until it expires. Because no endpoint here is\nidentity-gated, signing in is not required to read or advance the simulation.\n\n## Units and encodings\n\n- `temperature` — `Float`, degrees Celsius (°C).\n- `humidity` — `Float`, percent (%).\n- `gas` — `Nat`, raw ADC value (unitless integer counts).\n- `timestamp` — `Int`, nanoseconds since the Unix epoch (`Time.now()`).\n- `temperatureStatus`, `humidityStatus`, `gasStatus` — `SensorStatus` variant:\n  `#Stable`, `#Warning`, or `#Unstable`.\n- `overallStatus` — `OverallStatus` variant with the same three tags; it is the\n  worst status across the three sensors.\n\nStatus is derived from predefined bands per sensor. For temperature and\nhumidity, values inside the normal band are `Stable`, values between the normal\nand warning bounds are `Warning`, and values beyond the warning bounds are\n`Unstable`. For gas, values at or below `gasNormalMax` are `Stable`, values up\nto `gasWarningMax` are `Warning`, and values above it are `Unstable`.\nIndependently, any reading whose change from the previous reading exceeds\n`fluctuationDelta` is marked `Unstable` even when it is within range.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\nThe canister starts with an empty history, so `getCurrentReading()` returns\n`null` until `advanceSimulation()` is called at least once. The frontend drives\nthe simulation by calling `advanceSimulation()` on a timer; each call appends\none reading and trims the window back to 60. Poll `getCurrentReading()` or\n`getRecentReadings(limit)` after each advance. There is no server-side timer —\nif no client calls `advanceSimulation()`, no new readings are produced.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n`advanceSimulation()` is **not idempotent**. Each successful call appends a new\nreading and advances the random walk, so retrying a call that already succeeded\nproduces an extra reading rather than replaying the same one. A call that traps\nor is rejected rolls back entirely and leaves state unchanged, so retrying a\nfailed call is safe. Do not retry a call whose result you did not observe\nwithout expecting an additional reading.\n\n## Errors, limits, and gotchas\n\n- `getRecentReadings` clamps `limit` to the retained window size; it never\n  traps on a large `limit`.\n- The history is bounded at 60 readings; older readings are discarded and are\n  not recoverable.\n- `getCurrentReading()` returns `null` (not an error) before the first advance.\n- Status values are computed at the moment a reading is created and stored with\n  it; they do not change retroactively when thresholds change (thresholds are\n  constants, so this is not currently observable).\n- The OQL `reading` entity is public: anyone can query the full retained\n  history through `execute(...)`.\n";
  };
};
